# Copy to .env.local and fill in. .env.local is git-ignored; this file is not, so
# never put a real secret here — the values below are the local stack's fixed
# development keys, which are identical on every machine and protect nothing.
#
# For local development, run `pnpm exec supabase start` and then
# `pnpm exec supabase status` to print these. They do not change between restarts,
# so the values below should work as-is.
#
# For the hosted project, take them from the Supabase dashboard under
# Project Settings -> API keys.

# The REST endpoint. http://127.0.0.1:54321 locally; https://<ref>.supabase.co hosted.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321

# The publishable key (`sb_publishable_...`), successor to the legacy `anon` JWT.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ on purpose: this key is meant to reach the browser, and row level
# security — not secrecy — is what protects the data behind it. Proxying reads through
# a server action to hide it would buy nothing.
#
# There is a second key in the dashboard, the secret key (`sb_secret_...`, formerly
# `service_role`). It bypasses RLS entirely, so every policy is decorative to anything
# holding it. It must never appear in this file, never carry a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and
# never be imported by a client component or by any module one can reach.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_ACJWlzQHlZjBrEguHvfOxg_3BJgxAaH
